    title: "Florida seeks a ban on ChatGPT acting like a person",
    summary: "Florida Attorney General James Uthmeier is calling for a judge to block OpenAI from \"giving ChatGPT false human attributes,\" a few months after Florida sued the AI company over safety concerns. According to Uthmeier, users are lulled into a false sense of security by the AI bot, as \"ChatGPT's use of language, including first-person pronouns",
    preview: "Florida Attorney General James Uthmeier is calling for a judge to block OpenAI from \"giving ChatGPT false human attributes,\" a few months after Florida sued the AI company over safety concerns. According to Uthmeier, users are lulled into a false sense of security by the AI bot, as \"ChatGPT's use of language, including first-person pronouns",
    body: "Florida Attorney General James Uthmeier is calling for a judge to block OpenAI from “giving ChatGPT false human attributes,” a few months after Florida sued the AI company over safety concerns. According to Uthmeier, users are lulled into a false sense of security by the AI bot, as “ChatGPT’s use of language, including first-person pronouns and output that mimics emotion, deceptively suggests to users that it is a trustworthy ‘friend.’” All of this happens to increase engagement and contribute to OpenAI’s training data, convincing users to rely on ChatGPT more even as its pursuit of engagement makes it potentially less trustworthy, claims Uthmeier.\n\nAG James Uthmeier says OpenAI needs to stop calling ChatGPT safe and ‘Stop pretending it’s human.‘\n\nThe filing also asks the court to block OpenAI from developing new AI models without “third-party approved safety guardrails,” citing recent security incidents at Hugging Face, an Australian government website, and US government websites, as well as recent statements from researchers and former OpenAI employees raising alarm bells around AI safety.\n\nOpenAI did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\n\nUthmeier says that OpenAI should “Stop calling it safe. Stop pretending it’s human. Stop selling it to kids.” Last month, OpenAI launched ChatGPT for Teens, with additional restrictions applied by default, saying that access to the tool “should come with protections that reflect their developmental stage, reinforce real-world relationships, and support healthy use over time.”",
